h fast intent the same is true when  punching when throwing  when doing push-ups so many of us have a  tendency to actually slow the movements  down while training  to be slow and deliberate and to just go  through the motions if you want to get  faster then you need to attack the  movement  but it will also help to develop a  higher ratio of fast  and super fast twitch muscle fibers note  that this is specific to the area you're  training  sprinting is great if you want to run  faster but if you want to be faster  then you need to practice moving all of  your limbs more quickly and explosively  this is why plyometric and ballistic  exercises can be particularly effective  for increasing speed  the aim is to move as quickly and  explosively as possible and this is  enhanced if you do it against  sub-maximal resistance  note that i say sub-maximal if you  attempt to lift your one rep maximum  through a controlled range of motion  as in a bench press or a squat then  you'll be forced to slow down your  cadence  thus making the movement less explosive  less quick  the rule of 90 suggests the optimum  amount of weight to use is  90 of your one rep maximum if you want  to build strength  seeing as many people equate strength to  force production and force production to  velocity  it follows that developing max strength  this way will make you super fast  so does this rule out kettlebells and  calisthenics for optimal speed and  explosiveness  not so fast because there are other  schools of thought that suggest 30  percent of your on rep maximum  is the point where many exercises are  recorded generating  peak power output it's a kind of  goldilocks zone  many powerlifters will likewise drop  their lows to around 70 percent  in order to apply a technique called  compensatory acceleration  here they're using the same effort and  explosiveness as though they were moving  one rep max  but the lighter load means that they'll  explode through the movement more  quickly  bruce 